existying main.tf
Since the EC2 instance and Elastic IP are already defined as resources in your main.tf, you should reference them directly instead of using data sources. You just need to add the aws_eip_association resource.

Here is your updated /home/bob/terraform/main.tf:

hcl
# Provision EC2 instance
resource "aws_instance" "ec2" {
  ami           = "ami-0c101f26f147fa7fd"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  subnet_id     = "subnet-73ddd3a4e428dba9b"
  vpc_security_group_ids = [
    "sg-7303c98ed011a2475"
  ]

  tags = {
    Name = "devops-ec2"
  }
}

# Provision Elastic IP
resource "aws_eip" "ec2_eip" {
  tags = {
    Name = "devops-ec2-eip"
  }
}

# Associate the Elastic IP with the EC2 instance
resource "aws_eip_association" "ec2_eip_assoc" {
  instance_id   = aws_instance.ec2.id
  allocation_id = aws_eip.ec2_eip.id
}
💡 Key Details
Direct References: aws_instance.ec2.id and aws_eip.ec2_eip.id refer to the resources already defined in your file, creating a clean dependency chain.

Association Resource: aws_eip_association links the EIP to the instance without managing either resource's lifecycle.

No Data Sources Needed: Since both resources are Terraform-managed in the same configuration, data lookups would be redundant and could fail.

Region: Handled by your existing provider.tf (us-east-1).

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-addresses --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=devops-ec2-eip"
You should see the InstanceId field populated with the ID of devops-ec2, confirming the association is active.
